# converted from 09-after.md
@title After
@bg field
@mood hope

narrate They buried Cael at the edge of the field, because there was nowhere else and nobody wanted him anywhere else.
narrate It was Bryn who dug it. He would not let anyone help. He dug for four hours in the rain at the edge of a hole in the ground and when it was done he stood up and wiped his hands on his coat and said, to nobody:
corin neutral Seventeen years.
narrate Then he put the cushion in the grave, and the wooden horse, and the ledger with Day 1. She is herself. She is herself. She is herself. written three times in the margin.
narrate And then he filled it in.
narrate The Order did not move.
narrate That was the strangest part of the whole week. Four thousand people in a fold of ground, a Class One prohibition spent, a hole in a field that had just been asked a question by a crowd — and the line on the ridge simply stood there, professionally, with a folding table, and did not advance, and did not fire, and did not write anything down.
narrate Corin walked up the ridge alone to find out why.
narrate The officer in command was a woman of about forty with a grey coat and a face like a closed door, and she looked at him for a long moment, and then she said:
bryn neutral The mandate is the Fever.
corin neutral Yes.
bryn neutral There is no Fever.
corin neutral No.
bryn neutral Then there is no mandate.
bryn neutral I have been in this service nineteen years and I have burned four villages and I have never once been able to say that sentence. I would like to go home.
narrate She turned to her men.
bryn neutral We're done,
narrate The line broke up over the next two days.
narrate Some of them went south to Ashford. Some of them simply walked off the ridge and into the Mire and asked, at the first village they came to, whether there was work. One of them — a young lieutenant with a cough he had been hiding — stayed with the Mire, and married a woman from the Hollow, and was still there eleven years later, and his children grew up thinking the Order was a story.
narrate The Coven came in on the fourth day.
narrate Thirty of them, and then sixty, and then more, walking out of the drowned villages north of the Hollow where Cael had kept them for twelve years. Corin had expected trouble. He had spent five years in the Order and he knew exactly what a leaderless cult does when its leader dies, and he had a plan for it, and the plan was mostly run.
narrate They buried Cael and then they asked him, one at a time, the same question.
narrate And Corin — who had no mark, no authority, and no idea — told them the truth, slowly, the way Cael had taught him without meaning to: that there was nothing to do, and that there was a point in the next hour, and that was the one they were working on.
narrate Most of them stayed. A few went back to the villages they had come from. One of them, a woman of sixty with a burned face who had been in the Coven longer than anyone, planted something on Cael's grave and never said what it was.
narrate The Coven did not become anything. That was the mercy of it. It simply stopped being a Coven and became thirty-odd people with gardens, which is what most of them had wanted in the first place.
narrate The eastern line came down on the ninth day.
narrate Not by order. There was nobody left to give one. A man named Osric — no relation, a cousin of Marta's — walked out to the rope with a knife and cut it, and forty thousand people discovered that the road had been open the whole time and that the only thing holding it shut had been a man with a ledger and a reason.
narrate Corin stayed in the Mire.
narrate He did not mean to. He had a plan, on the ridge, that involved going south and finding the Warden and doing something about the Order — but the Warden had gone south already, with a ledger under his arm and a commission he had resigned by walking out of a map room, and he wrote to Corin twice a year for eleven years and never once came back.
narrate So Corin stayed.
narrate He built a house on the high ground where Maeve's cottage had been, because it was the only ground for a mile that did not want to be a bog, and he planted a herb garden that had no business surviving, and it survived.
narrate He found out about his father in the third year.
narrate It was in the ledger, four pages back, in his own handwriting from a night in Ashford he did not remember: Bryn says his name was Halden and he was a cartwright and he died of a fever when I was four months old and my mother never spoke about him because it was easier. I have no memory of this. I am writing it down so that I will have it later.
corin neutral Halden.
narrate He read it eleven times. Then he went out to the bench where Bryn was sitting and said
narrate And Bryn — who had been waiting thirty years for someone to say that name — put down his cup and told him the whole of it, and it took an hour, and Corin did not remember a single word of it the next morning, and wrote it down anyway.
narrate He was not a soldier. He was not a healer. He was, as far as anyone could work out, the man who had ended the Fever, which meant that for eleven years people walked from villages forty miles away to stand in his garden and ask him things.
narrate He never once told them what to do. He told them what had happened, slowly, the way a healer tells a person about a wound — and by the eleventh year there was a whole generation in the Mire who had been told the story properly, and not one of them could have said whether he was a hero.
narrate That was the point. He had worked that out somewhere around the third year.
narrate Bryn stayed too.
narrate He did not explain why, and nobody asked, and he spent the rest of his life on the high ground in a chair with a dog at his feet and a ledger on his knee. Corin wrote the same four words about him every year, in the same place, at the bottom of the page.
narrate He never once asked Bryn about the road. Bryn never once offered. That was the whole of the arrangement and it held for as long as it needed to.
narrate And the faceless things were gone.
narrate Nobody saw them go, and nobody ever worked out where. They had been what was left of people who had paid everything at the hole in the field, and when the Fever ended and the deep place had what it had been given, there was nothing left to hold them anywhere.
narrate There were eleven of them in the Mire that first winter. By the spring there were none, and the last one anyone reported was standing at the edge of the field eleven miles north of nowhere, on the grass, at a very precise distance from the hole, doing nothing at all.
narrate Bryn said it looked like it was waiting for someone. Corin said that was a dangerous thing to say out loud, and wrote it down anyway.
narrate And the dog lived fourteen years, which is a very long time for a dog, and had four puppies, and one of them was still on the high ground eleven years later, lying in the exact spot where the old one had always lain, at a very precise distance from a hole in a field.

@ach the_last_dawn
@diary places the field

